1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,370
Hello!

2
00:00:01,370 --> 00:00:06,620
This is Ria’s Slow Korean Podcast.

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:12,770
Everyone, I’m going on a trip next week.

4
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:17,220
I’m going on a trip.

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:18,720
For a month

6
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:22,140
I’m going on a trip for a month.

7
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:28,510
So I went to the supermarket today.

8
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:31,110
I went to the supermarket.

9
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:38,600
I bought a lot of different things.

10
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:51,390
Today, I’m going to talk about a place I really love—the supermarket.

11
00:00:51,390 --> 00:01:01,600
And I’ll tell you what Koreans usually buy whenever they go to the supermarket.

12
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,520
Are you ready?

13
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:07,180
First up, my shopping cart!

14
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:10,720
I’m going to show you what’s in my shopping cart.

15
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,620
I’ll show you.

16
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:17,340
Ta-da! Here’s the cart.

17
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,450
The cart is full.

18
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:23,240
It’s full, isn’t it?

19
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:26,210
Here are some snacks.

20
00:01:26,210 --> 00:01:30,050
There are multigrain cookies.

21
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:39,330
The first thing I do when I go to the supermarket is check out the snack aisle.

22
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:42,880
I check out the snack aisle.

23
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:50,630
There are so many new snack products in Korea these days.

24
00:01:50,630 --> 00:01:55,120
“Sin-sang” means they’ve just been released.

25
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:59,710
Newly released products.

26
00:01:59,710 --> 00:02:03,030
Newly released snacks.

27
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:08,940
Dubai Chewy Cookies were really popular in Korea.

28
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:16,900
So other companies are copying these Chewy Cookies.

29
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:18,370
They’re copying them.

30
00:02:18,370 --> 00:02:21,120
They’ve started copying them.

31
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:26,030
I check out these new snacks.

32
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:27,360
I check them out.

33
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:34,700
And I search for them online right there.

34
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:41,510
There are tons of snack reviews on the Korean internet.

35
00:02:41,510 --> 00:02:48,340
If there are a lot of reviews saying it tastes bad, I put the snack back.

36
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:51,190
I put it back.

37
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:56,480
Do you guys know the expression “having thin ears”?

38
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,680
Having thin ears.

39
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:05,340
It doesn’t mean that your ears are actually physically thin.

40
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:18,890
“Having thin ears” refers to someone who easily believes what others say and changes their mind quickly.

41
00:03:18,890 --> 00:03:33,680
For example, if a friend says, “I like this,” they’ll buy it right away, but if another friend says, “It’s not that great,” they’ll change their mind just as quickly.

42
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:37,380
I’m not usually like that.

43
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:44,830
But when it comes to buying snacks, I become easily swayed.

44
00:03:44,830 --> 00:03:48,090
I trust reviews way too much.

45
00:03:48,090 --> 00:03:52,680
I trust snack reviews way too much.

46
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:58,690
By the way, the snack I bought isn’t a new release.

47
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:01,300
It’s not a new product.

48
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:05,080
It’s a snack my dad likes.

49
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:07,930
So I just bought them.

50
00:04:07,930 --> 00:04:11,510
And I bought some nuts.

51
00:04:11,510 --> 00:04:13,250
Nuts!

52
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:15,360
What are nuts?

53
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,670
I’ll give you a hint.

54
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:22,430
Peanuts

55
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:25,440
Walnuts

56
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:28,490
This last one is really easy.

57
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:31,080
Almonds

58
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:34,980
All of these are called nuts.

59
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:36,610
Nuts

60
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:39,910
Do you like nuts?

61
00:04:39,910 --> 00:04:43,690
I really love nuts.

62
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:49,070
I like to eat yogurt with nuts in it.

63
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:53,680
I like to eat yogurt with nuts in it.

64
00:04:53,680 --> 00:05:02,720
But I’m currently getting braces, so I can’t chew very well.

65
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,790
They’re hard, so I can’t chew them well.

66
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:08,130
I’m so sad...

67
00:05:08,130 --> 00:05:13,990
But my mom really loves nuts, so I bought some.

68
00:05:13,990 --> 00:05:17,560
I bought two bags of nuts.

69
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:24,320
What do Koreans buy the most at the supermarket?

70
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:29,420
There’s a term in Korean called “must-have items.”

71
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:31,270
필수템

72
00:05:31,270 --> 00:05:36,540
“필수” means something you absolutely must have.

73
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:38,120
Absolutely

74
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:43,000
“템” comes from the English word “item.”

75
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,260
So “필수템” is a kind of slang.

76
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:51,190
It’s a term that’s used a lot.

77
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:55,000
Koreans’ must-have supermarket item!

78
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,540
The must-have supermarket item is none other than ramen.

79
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:01,810
Instant noodles!

80
00:06:01,810 --> 00:06:10,240
But Korean instant noodles are now very easy to find abroad.

81
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:13,180
You can get them easily.

82
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:16,540
We export ramen.

83
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:18,060
Export

84
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:20,220
To export.

85
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,440
This Shin Ramyun is so famous.

86
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:29,040
We export Shin Ramyun overseas.

87
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:35,280
So, how do you handle Korean ramen in your country?

88
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:38,880
On the contrary, they import it.

89
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:40,530
They import it.

90
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:41,980
Import

91
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:44,040
Import.

92
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:47,010
Do you guys like Shin Ramyun?

93
00:06:47,010 --> 00:06:49,060
I’m really curious!

94
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:55,020
This Shin Ramyun is the most famous ramen in Korea.

95
00:06:55,020 --> 00:07:00,940
Hmm... What’s your favorite cup noodle?

96
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:06,750
Anyway, you can easily find Shin Ramyun abroad.

97
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:08,810
It’s easy to find.

98
00:07:08,810 --> 00:07:12,800
But why did I buy it?

99
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:15,680
It's for emergencies.

100
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:17,540
For emergencies

101
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,420
It’s for emergencies.

102
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:24,580
“비상용” means you use it when you’re in a hurry.

103
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:27,360
You use it when you’re in a hurry.

104
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:35,960
For example, let’s say I took a flight and arrived in a foreign country.

105
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:44,470
But I’m so tired that I don’t have the energy to go to the supermarket.

106
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:51,040
That’s when I think of my emergency cup noodles.

107
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:53,170
I had this!!

108
00:07:53,170 --> 00:07:57,060
I bought this cup noodle in Korea!!

109
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:59,960
Oh, thank goodness... thank goodness...

110
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:02,330
Thank you so much.

111
00:08:02,330 --> 00:08:04,190
Thank you so much.

112
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:05,630
Thank you?

113
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:07,390
Who are you thanking?

114
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:09,220
Thanking myself? (lol)

115
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:14,280
Anyway, I eat cup ramen while doing this.

116
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:17,010
And then I sleep.

117
00:08:17,010 --> 00:08:18,450
I rest.

118
00:08:18,450 --> 00:08:21,480
This is my emergency supply.

119
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,490
Emergency cup noodles!

120
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:29,020
And I bought some pho cup noodles.

121
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:33,220
These are hard to find overseas.

122
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:34,400
Or am I wrong?

123
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:35,580
Is it easy to find?

124
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:45,550
There are pho cup noodles like this—have any of you ever seen pho cup noodles in your country?

125
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:51,840
I thought these might be hard to find, so I bought them in advance.

126
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,930
I bought them in advance.

127
00:08:53,930 --> 00:09:02,170
If they’re available in your country, I won’t buy them anymore.

128
00:09:02,170 --> 00:09:04,200
I just won’t buy them anymore.

129
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:09,410
But this cup noodle with rice noodles is really delicious.

130
00:09:09,410 --> 00:09:10,630
Rice

131
00:09:10,630 --> 00:09:15,730
Since it’s made with rice, it’s more filling than ramen.

132
00:09:15,730 --> 00:09:18,210
Ramen is made with wheat flour.

133
00:09:18,210 --> 00:09:22,530
Rice is more filling than flour.

134
00:09:22,530 --> 00:09:25,070
It really fills you up.

135
00:09:25,070 --> 00:09:28,600
And this rice noodle soup is spicy.

136
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:31,420
It’s spicier than Shin Ramyun.

137
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:33,790
It’s way spicier.

138
00:09:33,790 --> 00:09:38,640
I really love spicy pho, everyone!

139
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:46,550
There are lots of recipes in Korea that use this cup noodle version of pho.

140
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:55,600
And the second must-have item for Koreans at the supermarket is none other than seaweed!

141
00:09:55,600 --> 00:09:56,960
Seaweed

142
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:00,960
There are many types of seaweed in Korea.

143
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:05,750
The most famous type is square seaweed.

144
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:07,330
Square seaweed

145
00:10:07,330 --> 00:10:11,710
But I bought some shredded seaweed.

146
00:10:11,710 --> 00:10:13,380
Dried Seaweed Flakes

147
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:17,730
This feels really crumbly.

148
00:10:17,730 --> 00:10:19,020
Crumbs

149
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:24,020
This is delicious when sprinkled on top of rice.

150
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:25,850
Sprinkle it like this.

151
00:10:25,850 --> 00:10:29,060
Then it’s really savory(salty).

152
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:30,400
It’s savory(salty).

153
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:34,940
So you don’t need any other side dishes.

154
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:37,090
You don’t need any other side dishes.

155
00:10:37,090 --> 00:10:43,780
Just plain white rice and this seaweed side dish—that’s all I need for a meal!

156
00:10:43,780 --> 00:10:49,010
When I’m really too lazy to cook, this is all I eat.

157
00:10:49,010 --> 00:10:52,380
When I’m really too lazy, this is all I eat.

158
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:56,060
And I bought some seaweed snacks, too.

159
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,960
Seaweed crackers

160
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:01,940
They look like seaweed, but they’re actually crackers.

161
00:11:01,940 --> 00:11:07,240
There are lots of these seaweed crackers in Thailand too, right?

162
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:10,740
There are lots of seaweed crackers in Thailand.

163
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:17,120
Alright! What is the third must-have item for Koreans?

164
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:20,640
Take a guess, everyone!

165
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:23,900
It’s a very famous food.

166
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:29,540
It’s the food that immediately comes to mind when you think of Korea.

167
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:31,420
Shall I give you a hint?

168
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:33,250
It starts with “kim.”

169
00:11:33,250 --> 00:11:34,390
Kim

170
00:11:34,390 --> 00:11:38,690
It’s not the “kim” I mentioned earlier.

171
00:11:38,690 --> 00:11:41,380
Kim...ch..

172
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:42,450
Kimchi.

173
00:11:42,450 --> 00:11:43,870
Kimchi!

174
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:49,010
You can easily find kimchi abroad, too.

175
00:11:49,010 --> 00:11:55,340
But not just regular kimchi—more like stir-fried kimchi.

176
00:11:55,340 --> 00:11:57,150
Stir-fried kimchi

177
00:11:57,150 --> 00:12:03,330
It’s nice to have small packs of stir-fried kimchi like this.

178
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:11,420
Okay, so today we’ve talked about the must-have items for Koreans at the supermarket.

179
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:14,320
First, ramen.

180
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:17,130
Second, seaweed.

181
00:12:17,130 --> 00:12:19,780
Third, seaweed.

182
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:25,290
Sometimes we even eat all three of these at the same time.

183
00:12:25,290 --> 00:12:27,200
I eat them all at the same time.

184
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:30,510
I eat these three things at the same time.

185
00:12:30,510 --> 00:12:34,210
I put seaweed on top of the ramen.

186
00:12:34,210 --> 00:12:37,780
And I eat it with kimchi.

187
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:41,600
It really is the soul food of Koreans.

188
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:42,990
Soul food

189
00:12:42,990 --> 00:12:46,300
I couldn’t live without this.

190
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:47,870
I couldn’t live without it.

191
00:12:47,870 --> 00:12:51,410
What’s your soul food?

192
00:12:51,410 --> 00:13:02,340
What’s the one thing you always buy at the supermarket before going on a trip?

193
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:07,460
I’m so curious about your travel essentials!

194
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:10,720
Please share lots of comments.

195
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:17,590
Thank you so much for listening to my podcast today!

196
00:13:17,590 --> 00:13:31,190
Have a wonderful day. Bye!
